---
title: Specimen Parts
sidebar:
  label: Specimen parts
  order: 6
---

Use `Specimen Parts` for voucher preparations, tissues, swabs, and other
physical parts derived from the specimen. Each specimen can have multiple
parts. Organisms found living on or in the specimen are recorded separately as
[symbionts](#symbionts).

The symbiont tab is available for mammals, birds, herpetofauna, and fishes. It
is not available for invertebrate catalogs. For a fish lot with recorded
individuals, parts and symbionts can be linked to the individuals they came
from; see [Fishes](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/fishes/#parts-and-symbionts).

Part fields include:

- `Tissue ID`: optional tissue identifier. NAHPU can repeat or generate the next
  configured tissue number.
- `Individuals`: shown only for a fish lot with recorded individuals. Links the
  part to one or more individuals in the lot.
- `QR/barcode ID`: optional unique ID for a tube, tag, or container. On mobile,
  NAHPU can scan a QR/barcode; it can also generate a UUID.
- `Preparation type`: user-defined specimen part type.
- `Counts`: number of parts or containers.
- `Treatment`: preservation treatment.
- `Additional treatment`: optional second treatment.
- `Storage type`: storage medium or method.
- `Storage location`: freezer, cabinet, shelf, or container location.
- `Time taken`: time the part was taken, recommended for fresh tissues.
- `Preparator`: part-level preparator when different from the voucher preparator.
- `Date taken`: date the part was taken.
- `PMI`: post-mortem interval, for example `1:30`.
- `Museum permanent`: museum that permanently holds the part.
- `Museum loan`: museum receiving the part on loan.
- `Remarks`: notes specific to the part.

The part list displays the part type, count, barcode ID, preparator, tissue ID,
treatment, date/time, PMI, and whether a remark exists.

## Symbionts

A symbiont is any organism living on or in the host specimen, whatever its
relationship to the host: parasitic, mutualistic, commensal, phoretic, and so
on. Two fields describe the association:

- `Interaction type`: how the symbiont relates to its host. The default
  vocabulary is `Parasitic`, `Parasitoid`, `Mutualistic`, `Commensal`,
  `Phoretic`, and `Unknown`. Records created before NAHPU tracked symbionts
  were migrated with the interaction type `Parasitic`.
- `Category`: where the symbiont lives on its host. The defaults are
  `Ectosymbiont`, `Endosymbiont`, and `Mesosymbiont`.

On the specimen itself, `Symbionts examined` and `Symbionts detected` record
whether the host was checked and whether anything was found, so an examination
with no symbionts is still documented.

Symbiont records separate collection from identification. `Collector` is who
recovered the symbiont and starts as the host's preparator, or its cataloger when
no preparator is set. `Determiner`, `Date determined`, and `Determination method`
record who identified the symbiont, when, and how. The method uses the same
vocabulary as the specimen ID method. Choosing a `Category` narrows the
`Anatomical location` list to sites that fit it. Category values stored before
the rename, such as `Ectoparasite` and `Endoparasite`, narrow the list the same
way. Locations you added yourself always stay in the list.

Symbiont curation records carry their own `Storage` and `Storage location`
fields so the preservation method and physical location can be recorded
separately.

In Darwin Core exports, the interaction type becomes
`organismInteractionType`. A record without an interaction type falls back to
its category. When an interaction type is set, the category and association
status go into `organismInteractionDescription`.
